{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalkDegreeBound","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalkDegreeBound","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Polynomial.Laurent","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Span.Defs"],"declarations":11,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.one_mem_spanRange_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.one_mem_spanRange_zero","kind":"theorem","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LaurentWalkDegreeBound.lean#L161-L168","doc":"The identity element sits at the origin of the grading: a walk of zero\nsteps has zero degree span. Recorded separately because it is the base case\nthe linear bound is anchored on. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.exists_walk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.exists_walk","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LaurentWalkDegreeBound.lean#L149-L159","doc":"**The hypothesis of `walk_mem_spanRange` is satisfiable.**\n\nThe pure-`h` walk `f k = h^k` has exactly the required shape (`c = 0`,\n`d = 1`), so the degree bound is not vacuously true for want of any walk\nmeeting its premise. Recorded because a bound whose hypothesis nothing\nsatisfies would prove nothing about the probe. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.walk_mem_spanRange","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.walk_mem_spanRange","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LaurentWalkDegreeBound.lean#L125-L147","doc":"**The walk's degree span grows linearly in the number of steps.**\n\nAfter `n` steps from the identity `q⁰`, every exponent lies in `[-n, n]`.\nSo a walk of `n` crossings carries at most `2n + 1` distinct monomials per\ncoefficient: the *state* does not grow with the nucleon number, only the\npolynomial degree does, and only linearly in the crossing count.\n\nThe hypothesis `hstep` is where the atom path's restricted multipliers enter.\nA walk that multiplied by a general polynomial would not satisfy it, and the\nconclusion would genuinely fail — the bound is not an artifact of the\nstatement. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.step_mem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.step_mem","kind":"theorem","line":110,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LaurentWalkDegreeBound.lean#L110-L123","doc":"**One normal-form step widens the window by at most one on each side.**\n\nThe step is `new = c · old + d · (h · old)`: the two multiplier shapes the\natom path actually uses (`1` and `h`, each scaled by a ring element — `0`,\n`-1` and `-1/2` in the walk itself). No general polynomial multiplication\nappears, and that is what bounds the growth. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.C_mul_mem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.C_mul_mem","kind":"theorem","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LaurentWalkDegreeBound.lean#L101-L108","doc":"Scaling by a coefficient keeps the window. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.hQ_mul_mem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.hQ_mul_mem","kind":"theorem","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LaurentWalkDegreeBound.lean#L85-L99","doc":"**Multiplying by `h` widens the exponent window by exactly one on each\nside.** Together with `hQ_mul_T` this is the whole degree-growth story. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.spanRange_mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.spanRange_mono","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LaurentWalkDegreeBound.lean#L78-L83","doc":"A wider window contains a narrower one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.T_mem_spanRange","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.T_mem_spanRange","kind":"theorem","line":74,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LaurentWalkDegreeBound.lean#L74-L76","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.spanRange","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.spanRange","kind":"def","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LaurentWalkDegreeBound.lean#L70-L72","doc":"The `R`-submodule spanned by the monomials `qᵉ` with `a ≤ e ≤ b`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.hQ_mul_T","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.hQ_mul_T","kind":"theorem","line":57,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LaurentWalkDegreeBound.lean#L57-L68","doc":"**Multiplying a monomial by `h` is a shift-and-subtract.**\n\n`h · qᵉ = qᵉ⁺¹ - qᵉ⁻¹`: the exponent moves by `±1` and nothing else happens.\nThis is why the normal-form walk needs no general polynomial multiplication —\nevery step is a shift plus a scalar, so the cost is linear in the number of\nmonomials rather than quadratic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.hQ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.LaurentWalk.hQ","kind":"def","line":54,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/LaurentWalkDegreeBound.lean#L54-L55","doc":"The Hecke quantum integer `h = q - q⁻¹`, as a Laurent polynomial. ","signatureChunk":0}]}